WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:11.840
Prior to 1906, when the Pure Food and Drug&nbsp;
Act was passed, there were no regulations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:17.680
regarding the ethical use of human subjects in&nbsp;
research. There were no consumer regulations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:23.120
no Food and Drug Administration (FDA), no Common&nbsp;
Rule, and no Institutional Review Board (IRB).

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:26.480
Nuremberg Code.
A well-known chapter in the history of research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:33.280
with human subjects opened on December 9, 1946,&nbsp;
when an American military tribunal opened criminal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:38.480
proceedings against 23 leading German physicians&nbsp;
and administrators for their willing participation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:44.480
in war crimes and crimes against humanity. Among&nbsp;
the charges were that German physicians conducted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:48.960
medical experiments on thousands of concentration&nbsp;
camp prisoners without their consent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:54.400
Most of the subjects of these experiments&nbsp;
died or were permanently crippled as a result.

00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:00.400
As a direct result of the trial, the&nbsp;
Nuremberg Code was established in 1948,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:05.440
stating that "The voluntary consent of the human&nbsp;
subject is absolutely essential," making it clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:09.920
that subjects should give consent and that the&nbsp;
benefits of research must outweigh the risks.

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:14.560
Although it did not carry the force&nbsp;
of law, the Nuremberg Code was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:19.440
first international document which advocated&nbsp;
voluntary participation and informed consent.

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:23.360
Thalidomide
In the late 1950s, thalidomide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:28.880
was approved as a sedative in Europe; it was&nbsp;
not approved in the United States by the FDA.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:34.560
The drug was prescribed to control sleep and&nbsp;
nausea throughout pregnancy, but it was soon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:39.840
found that taking this drug during pregnancy&nbsp;
caused severe deformities in the fetus. Many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:45.040
patients did not know they were taking a drug that&nbsp;
was not approved for use by the FDA, nor did they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:51.360
give informed consent. Some 12,000 babies were&nbsp;
born with severe deformities due to thalidomide.

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:57.680
U.S. Senate hearings followed and in 1962 the&nbsp;
so-called "Kefauver Amendments" to the Food,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:01.760
Drug and Cosmetic Act were passed&nbsp;
into law to ensure drug efficacy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:06.960
and greater drug safety. For the first&nbsp;
time, drug manufacturers were required&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:10.720
to prove to FDA the effectiveness of&nbsp;
their products before marketing them.

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:14.320
Tuskegee Syphilis Study (1932-1972)&nbsp;

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:19.440
An equally well known chapter in history occurred&nbsp;
during a research project conducted by the U.S.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:25.600
Public Health Service. Six hundred low-income&nbsp;
African-American males, 400 of whom were infected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:31.680
with syphilis, were monitored for 40 years.&nbsp;
Free medical examinations were given; however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:37.600
subjects were not told about their disease.&nbsp;
Even though a proven cure, penicillin, became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:43.920
available in the 1950s, the study continued until&nbsp;
1972 with participants being denied treatment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:49.840
In some cases, when subjects were diagnosed&nbsp;
as having syphilis by other physicians,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:55.520
researchers intervened to prevent treatment.&nbsp;
Many subjects died of syphilis during the study.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:02.000
The study was stopped in 1973 by the U.S.&nbsp;
Department of Health, Education, and Welfare.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:07.360
Declaration of Helsinki
In 1964, the World Medical Association established&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:13.360
recommendations guiding medical doctors in&nbsp;
biomedical research involving human subjects. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:18.880
Declaration governs international research ethics&nbsp;
and defines rules for "research combined with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:26.240
clinical care" and "non-therapeutic research."&nbsp;
The Declaration of Helsinki was revised in 1975,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:32.960
1983, 1989 and 1996 and is the basis&nbsp;
for Good Clinical Practices used today.

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:34.720
Issues addressed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:39.600
in the Declaration of Helsinki include:
• Research with humans should be based on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:45.520
results from laboratory and animal experimentation
• Research protocols should be reviewed by an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:49.920
independent committee prior to initiation
• Informed consent from research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:53.680
participants is necessary
• Research should be conducted by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:59.840
medically/scientifically qualified individuals
• Risks should not exceed benefits

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:01.360
The Belmont Report&nbsp;

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:07.280
Finally, The Belmont Report was published&nbsp;
in 1979, with attempts to summarize the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:11.600
basic ethical principles identified by the&nbsp;
Commission in the course of its deliberations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:16.960
The Report is a statement of the basic ethical&nbsp;
principles and guidelines that should assist in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:21.360
resolving the ethical problems that surround&nbsp;
the conduct of research with human subjects.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:28.080
The Belmont Report established three basic ethical&nbsp;
principles – respect for persons, beneficence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:41.040
and justice – which are the cornerstones for&nbsp;
the regulations involving human subjects.

